```java
String read(String path) {
    return Files.readString(Path.of(path));  // compile error: unhandled IOException
}
```

```java
// Unchecked: no throws needed, compiler doesn't complain
int half(int n) {
    if (n < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("n must be >= 0");
    return n / 2;
}

// Checked: must declare (or catch)
String read(String path) throws IOException {
    return Files.readString(Path.of(path));
}
```
*The checked `IOException` needs `throws`; the unchecked `IllegalArgumentException` needs nothing.*

```java
// IOException (checked) -- reading a file
String cfg = Files.readString(Path.of("app.conf"));        // throws IOException

// SQLException (checked) -- JDBC
try (Connection c = ds.getConnection()) { /* ... */ }       // throws SQLException

// InterruptedException (checked) -- blocking call
try {
    Thread.sleep(1000);
} catch (InterruptedException e) {
    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();   // restore the interrupt flag
}

// ClassNotFoundException (checked) -- reflection
Class<?> c = Class.forName("com.example.Plugin");           // throws ClassNotFoundException
```
*All five are checked because they model conditions outside your code where recovery makes sense.*

```java
void load() {
    // IOException is checked and neither caught nor declared
    String s = Files.readString(Path.of("a.txt"));
}
// error: unreported exception java.io.IOException;
//        must be caught or declared to be thrown
```

```java
// Option A -- handle it here
void load() {
    try {
        String s = Files.readString(Path.of("a.txt"));
    } catch (IOException e) {
        log.error("could not read", e);
    }
}

// Option B -- declare it and let the caller handle it
void load() throws IOException {
    String s = Files.readString(Path.of("a.txt"));
}
```
*Either option satisfies catch-or-declare; doing neither is a compile error.*

```java
// Spring translates SQLException -> unchecked DataAccessException
User findUser(Long id) { return jdbcTemplate.queryForObject(SQL, mapper, id); }
User getUser(Long id)  { return findUser(id); }   // no throws clause anywhere

// Lambdas/streams compose cleanly now:
ids.forEach(id -> findUser(id));   // compiles -- no checked exception
```
*Unchecked exceptions keep signatures clean, decouple layers, and work inside lambdas and streams.*

```java
try {
    return Files.readString(path);
} catch (IOException e) {
    // (a) swallowed -> caller has no idea it failed
    return "";
    // (b) or: throw new RuntimeException("read failed");  // cause dropped!
}
```

```java
// Custom wrap, cause preserved
String read(Path path) {
    try {
        return Files.readString(path);
    } catch (IOException e) {
        throw new UncheckedIOException(e);   // JDK type, keeps the cause
    }
}

// Now it composes inside a stream (which can't throw checked exceptions):
paths.stream().map(this::read).forEach(System.out::println);
```
*`UncheckedIOException` carries the original `IOException`; `getCause()` returns it for debugging.*

```java
while (running) {
    try {
        Thread.sleep(1000);
    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        // empty catch: interrupt flag already cleared, signal lost,
        // loop keeps running -- the thread can never be cancelled
    }
}
```

```java
while (running) {
    try {
        Thread.sleep(1000);
    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();  // restore the cleared flag
        break;                               // exit the loop cleanly
    }
}
// Higher-level code checking isInterrupted() can now also react.
```
*Catching clears the interrupt flag — restore it so the cancellation signal isn't lost.*
